\begin{abstract}
Inference of adaptive molecular evolution from coding sequences remains strongly dependent on multiple sequence alignment (MSA). However, alignment uncertainty, recombination, substitutional saturation, synonymous-rate variation, multinucleotide substitutions, insertion--deletion processes, and related forms of model misspecification can distort downstream selection analyses. Here I present the full methodological specification of \ProjectName{} (\ProjectExpansion{}), a simulation-trained framework for branch-site selection inference that is robust to alignment-induced artifacts and explicitly aware of saturation-driven information loss. The method is not optimized for any single alignment or any single codon model.

\ProjectName{} is built around six principles: large-scale simulator-truth training data with tracked ancestral histories; alignment ensembles generated with multiple codon-aware workflows; supervised multi-task learning that separates alignment-invariant signal from aligner-specific artifact; explicit saturation modeling from latent multiple-hit burden and observed proxies; calibration across null, hard-negative, and out-of-distribution regimes; and benchmarking against likelihood-based and alignment-uncertainty-aware baselines.

The framework also includes an auxiliary second-stage module for alignment-integrated ancestral sequence reconstruction (ASR) and protein-energy interpretation. This auxiliary layer is not primary evidence for positive selection. Instead, it provides mechanistic context for high-confidence \ProjectName{} hits by propagating alignment uncertainty into ancestral-state ensembles and quantifying how candidate substitutions alter predicted structural or energetic constraints.

This article is intentionally self-contained and written as an executable methods blueprint. It specifies the generative simulator, tensorization scheme, neural architecture, loss functions, calibration strategy, evaluation design, software modules, auxiliary ASR/energy components, expected file formats, and reporting schema. It also records the current engineering evidence so that future empirical claims are not confused with simulation-supervised validation.

The accompanying implementation has reached version \ProjectVersion{}. Development has now moved beyond direct whole-gene classification, which proved unstable under four-tier saturation stress tests, to an explicit branch-site truth workflow with conservative feature policy. A completed 100,000-family Apple Silicon/MPS validation used 25,000 simulated families per saturation tier, 8 taxa, 300 codons, and the production-fast alignment ensemble \texttt{identity}, MAFFT, \texttt{BABAPPAlign}, and MUSCLE. All 104 tier-stage markers completed, the cross-tier truth audit reported \texttt{explicit\_truth\_ok} for all tiers, and no proxy foreground-taxon labels were detected. Branch-site neural test AUROC values were 0.9986, 0.9970, 0.9945, and 0.9900 for low, moderate, high, and extreme saturation tiers, respectively. Branch-level aggregation all-split AUROC values were 0.9998, 0.9994, 0.9988, and 0.9979, while branch-to-gene aggregation all-split AUROC values were 0.9997, 0.9996, 0.9995, and 0.9994. Destructive shuffled-branch-label controls remained near random, but context-only ablation still raised a shortcut warning. These are strong simulation-oracle results, not empirical biological claims. The implementation now also includes a deployable simulation-trained conservative-branch-site package, empirical input quality control, out-of-distribution applicability gates, pilot-panel tooling, and a first guarded WRKY diagnostic pilot. Final empirical positive-selection claims remain blocked until simulation-matched calibration and external reference interpretation are complete.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

Comparative analyses of protein-coding sequences often infer natural selection through codon models in which the ratio of nonsynonymous to synonymous substitution rates, $\omega = d_N/d_S$, serves as a central summary of selective pressure \citep{muse1994,goldman1994,yang2007}. Branch, site, and branch-site models have made it possible to test hypotheses about lineage-specific and site-specific adaptive evolution, and the improved branch-site likelihood method \citep{zhang2005} in particular became a standard approach for detecting episodic positive selection. Random-effects extensions such as BS-REL, aBSREL, MEME, and BUSTED further broadened the inferential scope by allowing selection to vary across sites and lineages \citep{pond2011_bsrel,murrell2012_meme,smith2015_absrel,murrell2015_busted}. These methods are powerful and widely used, but they operate on a prerequisite that is typically treated as fixed: a single inferred alignment of homologous codons.

That prerequisite is not benign. Alignment errors can materially inflate false positive rates in positive-selection scans, especially when evolutionary divergence and insertion--deletion activity increase \citep{jordan2012_alignment,redelings2014_erasing}. Recombination can violate the assumption that one phylogeny describes the history of all sites, sometimes causing severe false positive behavior when ignored \citep{anisimova2003_recombination,scheffler2006_recombination}. Synonymous rate variation can yield large excesses of false positives if not modeled \citep{wisotsky2020_srv,davydov2019_ratevariation}. Multinucleotide substitutions and related non-canonical mutational processes can also mislead classical codon-model selection inference \citep{venkat2018_mnm,dunn2019_mnm,lucaci2023_mhs}. Substitutional saturation is a distinct confounder that must be handled explicitly: hidden multiple hits can erase historical signal, compress observed synonymous divergence, and drive branch-site tests toward low power or unstable interpretation, particularly for deeper divergences \citep{gharib2013_branchsite,chi2020_clusters,duchene2022_saturation,xia2003_iss}. Recent work such as BUSTED-E demonstrates that even heavily curated genome-scale alignments may retain residual local errors severe enough to distort inference, motivating explicit modeling of artifact-like regimes \citep{selberg2025_bustede}. At the same time, the first deep-learning selection studies have shown that simulation-trained neural models can, in principle, learn useful signals from aligned sequence data \citep{west2025_omegaai}. The field is therefore ready for a new class of methods that does not discard codon-model reasoning, but no longer treats a single MSA as unquestioned truth and no longer treats highly saturated sequence space as equally informative as lightly diverged sequence space.

The objective of \ProjectName{} is to address this problem directly. The framework is designed for robust branch-site selection inference under alignment uncertainty by training on large ensembles of simulated orthogroups for which the full latent evolutionary history is known. Rather than asking a model to imitate the output of CODEML or any other existing program, \ProjectName{} uses simulator-truth labels and explicitly exposes the learner to confounding processes that often generate false or distorted selection signals in practice. Multiple aligners are run on the same simulated family, creating several imperfect homology hypotheses for one underlying evolutionary history. The model is then trained to identify which signal patterns are consistent with true branch-site selection and which are specific to one aligner, one artifact class, or one misspecified regime.

This paper is written as a complete methods specification rather than as a minimal software note. Its purpose is twofold. First, it defines the scientific and statistical target of \ProjectName{} with enough precision that later performance claims can be judged against an explicit design. Second, it is intended to be implementation-ready: a new research assistant, developer, or coding agent should be able to use this document alone to build the simulator, data pipeline, training stack, benchmarking scripts, and reporting workflow. Where information is not yet available because the downstream execution has not yet been run, explicit placeholders are provided instead of silent omissions.

A second conceptual point is equally important. Ancestral reconstruction and biophysical landscape analysis are biologically informative, but they do not by themselves constitute a primary statistical test of positive selection. Recent work shows that integrating alignment uncertainty can improve ASR accuracy and downstream structural inference, and that modern ASR formulations can incorporate richer sequence models and epistatic information \citep{aadland2020_asr,chisholm2024_energy,deleonardis2025_ardca,muniztrejo2025_robustasr}. For that reason, the present manuscript incorporates ASR and protein-energy analysis only as an auxiliary interpretation layer that operates after robust branch-site candidates have been identified. This preserves a clear separation between \emph{selection inference} and \emph{mechanistic interpretation}, which I argue is the most defensible way to unite the two directions within a single methods paper.

\section{Conceptual framing: simulator-truth, observational truth, and identifiability}

A central problem in this area is misuse of the phrase ``ground truth.'' In real comparative genomic datasets, the true historical locations of adaptive events are generally unknown. Accordingly, \ProjectName{} distinguishes three inferential layers.

\textbf{Simulator-truth} refers to the latent evolutionary history used to generate synthetic orthogroups: the true branch tree or local segment trees, true codon homology map, true mutation events, true insertion and deletion events, true recombination breakpoints, and the true subset of branch-site pairs assigned to adaptive regimes. Only simulation provides this level of truth.

\textbf{Analysis truth} refers to the output of a downstream method applied to a particular representation of the data, such as a single alignment analyzed by CODEML, MEME, or aBSREL. Analysis truth is not historical truth; it is method-specific inference.

\textbf{Observational identifiability} refers to whether the available extant sequences contain sufficient information to recover a meaningful approximation to the simulator-truth signal. Some branch-site events are intrinsically difficult to recover once indels, short branches, synonymous-site saturation, codon-position-specific multiple hits, or recombination obscure site homology and event localization. In BABAPPA, severe saturation is not merely another nuisance covariate; it is treated as a mechanism of information destruction that can render a confident branch-site call scientifically indefensible even when a classifier remains numerically decisive.

For this reason, \ProjectName{} is framed as a \emph{robust branch-site inference system} rather than an oracle that can always recover exact historical selection. The framework is trained to predict simulator-truth labels, but it also learns an \emph{artifact probability} and an \emph{out-of-distribution or low-identifiability probability}. This distinction is essential. A method that forces every ambiguous case into a positive-selection call may achieve attractive sensitivity in some simulation slices while being scientifically unusable on real data.

