\section{Generative simulator for orthogroup evolution}

\subsection{General requirements}

The simulator is the scientific core of \ProjectName{}. If its generative assumptions are narrow, the trained model will merely memorize one stylized world. Therefore the simulator must support broad, randomized, and explicitly parameterized variation across tree shapes, sequence lengths, codon compositions, background selection regimes, indel processes, recombination intensity, synonymous-rate variation, multinucleotide mutations, and branch-site adaptive events.

Each synthetic family $g$ is represented by a tuple
\[
\mathcal{D}_g = \left(T_g,\ \mathcal{A}_g,\ \mathcal{H}_g,\ \mathcal{Y}_g,\ \Theta_g\right),
\]
where $T_g$ is the species tree or segment-wise local trees, $\mathcal{A}_g$ is the ancestral history, $\mathcal{H}_g$ is the tracked codon homology map, $\mathcal{Y}_g$ is the set of truth labels, and $\Theta_g$ is the vector of generative parameters.

\subsection{Tree generation}

For each family, the simulator draws a rooted phylogeny $T_g=(V_g,E_g)$ with $n_g$ taxa. The simplest default is a Yule pure-birth tree, but the framework should support parametric trees (Yule or birth--death with sampled branch lengths), template trees rescaled from empirical orthogroups, and hybrid trees that combine empirical topologies with randomized branch-length perturbations.

The number of taxa $n_g$ should be sampled over a broad range, for example
\[
n_g \sim \text{DiscreteUniform}(8, 64),
\]
unless an experiment fixes the value. Branch lengths should be drawn under a mixture distribution so that shallow, moderate, and deep divergences are all represented. A practical default is to sample a mean tree depth and then rescale branches with branch-specific multipliers:
\[
\ell_b = \tau_g \cdot \rho_b,
\]
where $\tau_g$ controls family-level divergence and $\rho_b$ is a branch-specific rate multiplier.

\subsection{Root sequence generation}

Each family begins with a root coding sequence of length $L_g$ codons:
\[
L_g \sim \text{DiscreteUniform}(100, 2000),
\]
or from an empirical length distribution if desired. Root codons can be generated either by a parametric root procedure (codons sampled from stationary frequencies $\bm{\pi}_g$) or by a template-root procedure (codons sampled from empirical coding regions while preserving amino-acid composition and codon bias).

To encourage realism, codon frequencies should be family-specific:
\[
\bm{\pi}_g \sim \text{Dirichlet}(\bm{\alpha}_{\pi}),
\]
with optional GC and codon-usage constraints.

Each root codon is assigned an immutable latent homology identifier. When insertion events occur later, newly inserted codons receive new identifiers together with a birth branch and birth time. This explicit homology bookkeeping is crucial because it makes training labels unambiguous even when downstream alignments disagree.

\subsection{Background codon evolution}

The background substitution process can be expressed as an MG94- or GY94-style codon model \citep{muse1994,goldman1994}. For branch $b$ and site $i$, define an instantaneous rate matrix
\[
Q_{b,i} = \rho_b \alpha_i \sigma_i Q_{\mathrm{codon}}\!\left(\kappa_g,\bm{\pi}_g,\omega_{b,i},\eta_g\right),
\]
where $\rho_b$ is the branch multiplier, $\alpha_i$ is the site-specific rate multiplier, $\sigma_i$ is an optional synonymous-rate modifier, $\kappa_g$ is the transition/transversion bias, $\bm{\pi}_g$ is the codon-frequency vector, $\omega_{b,i}$ is the branch-site nonsynonymous pressure, and $\eta_g$ controls the probability of multinucleotide events when the generalized model is used.

The site-specific factors should be sampled from broad distributions, e.g.
\[
\alpha_i \sim \text{Gamma}(k_\alpha,\theta_\alpha), \qquad
\sigma_i \sim \text{LogNormal}(\mu_\sigma,\tau_\sigma^2).
\]
This allows pervasive rate heterogeneity and synonymous-rate variation, both of which are known to affect selection inference \citep{wisotsky2020_srv,davydov2019_ratevariation}.

\subsection{Branch-site adaptive events}

The core labels of interest are branch-site adaptive events. For each family, a subset of branches is assigned as event-eligible:
\[
Z^{(\mathrm{branch})}_{g,b} \in \{0,1\}.
\]
For eligible branches, a subset of latent homologous codon identifiers is selected as adaptive targets:
\[
Z^{(\mathrm{site})}_{g,b,h} \in \{0,1\}, \qquad h \in \mathcal{H}_g.
\]
The adaptive regime can then be imposed by setting
\[
\omega_{b,h} =
\begin{cases}
\omega_{\mathrm{bg},h} & \text{if } Z^{(\mathrm{site})}_{g,b,h}=0,\\
\omega_{\mathrm{sel},g,b,h} & \text{if } Z^{(\mathrm{site})}_{g,b,h}=1,
\end{cases}
\]
with $\omega_{\mathrm{sel},g,b,h}>1$ and $\omega_{\mathrm{bg},h}\le 1$ unless a relaxed-constraint regime is being simulated separately.

To avoid an unrealistically simplistic binary world, adaptive events should vary in strength ($\omega_{\mathrm{sel}}$), duration (entire branch versus branch segment), prevalence (fraction of sites affected), clustering (independent sites versus contiguous hotspots), and recurrence (single-branch versus repeated or convergent events).

The simulator must also include difficult near-null scenarios such as short foreground branches, weak positive selection, and very sparse selected sites.

\subsection{Insertion and deletion processes}

Indels are indispensable because alignment uncertainty is one of the main targets of the framework. The simulator must therefore record not only final sequences, but also the true history of insertion and deletion events and the resulting homology map. Indels can be generated under a length-dependent distribution as in INDELible \citep{fletcher2009_indelible}, but the exact mechanism is configurable. A practical default is:
\[
N_{\mathrm{indel},b} \sim \text{Poisson}(\lambda_{\mathrm{indel},g}\ell_b),
\]
with lengths sampled from a geometric or Zipf-like distribution. Insertions and deletions should be parameterized separately because asymmetric processes are common in real datasets.

\subsection{Recombination and local genealogies}

Because recombination can invalidate the single-tree assumption of codon-model inference \citep{anisimova2003_recombination,scheffler2006_recombination}, \ProjectName{} includes recombination as an explicit nuisance process. The simulator should support two practical modes.

\textbf{Mode 1: breakpoint mosaic simulation.} The coding sequence is partitioned into contiguous segments, each segment using its own local tree obtained by nearest-neighbor-interchange or subtree-prune-regraft perturbation of the global tree.

\textbf{Mode 2: ARG-inspired approximation.} Breakpoints are sampled along the sequence and local genealogies are generated from a simplified ancestral recombination graph approximation.

Each family stores breakpoint locations and local trees. Importantly, recombination is \emph{not} treated as positive selection, but as a potential confounder that may create false or fragmented branch-site signal in downstream analyses.

\subsection{Substitutional saturation as an explicit confounder}

Saturation cannot be handled adequately by simply allowing long branches in the simulator. In this context, saturation refers to the accumulation of repeated substitutions at the same historical positions such that the observed alignment no longer preserves a faithful record of the underlying number, type, and temporal order of changes \citep{xia2003_iss,duchene2022_saturation}. For branch-site inference, the most relevant consequence is not only general information loss, but specifically the erosion or compression of synonymous divergence, local codon-state history, and sitewise branch attribution \citep{gharib2013_branchsite,chi2020_clusters}.

Accordingly, \ProjectName{} treats saturation at two distinct levels.

\textbf{Latent saturation.} Because the simulator tracks the full mutational history, it can compute branch-, segment-, and family-level multiple-hit burden directly from the generative record. A practical latent statistic is
\[
S^{(\mathrm{latent})}_{g,r} =
\frac{\#\{\text{historical nucleotide changes in region }r\} - \#\{\text{observable terminal differences in }r\}}{\max\left(1,\#\{\text{historical nucleotide changes in region }r\}\right)},
\]
where region $r$ may denote a family, segment, codon-position class, or branch-conditioned window. Values near zero indicate little hidden change, whereas values near one indicate strong masking of historical substitutions.

\textbf{Observed saturation proxies.} Real data do not provide latent histories, so the simulator must also emit observed diagnostics that a deployed method could compute. Recommended proxies include: Xia's index of substitution saturation (Iss) or its codon-position-specific analogues \citep{xia2003_iss}; entropy-based saturation scores \citep{duchene2022_saturation}; transition/transversion flattening curves; pairwise uncorrected-versus-corrected distance residuals; branch-length compression relative to simulated truth; and position-specific estimates of synonymous divergence exhaustion.

Simulation of saturation should be decoupled from simple divergence depth by randomizing several contributing factors, including mean tree depth and branch-length heterogeneity, transition/transversion ratio, codon-position-specific rate multipliers, among-site rate variation, GC or compositional shifts interacting with multiple-hit processes, segment-specific acceleration under recombination, and foreground events embedded within otherwise deeply diverged backgrounds.

Families should then be stratified into at least four saturation tiers according to latent and observed summaries: low, moderate, high, and extreme. Extreme-saturation families are especially important because the scientifically appropriate behavior may be abstention rather than forced prediction. The objective of \ProjectName{} is therefore not to ``correct'' saturation away in all cases, but to learn the boundary between recoverable and nonrecoverable branch-site signal under saturation pressure.

\subsection{Multinucleotide substitutions and model misspecification}

Single-nucleotide codon transitions are insufficient for realism. Multinucleotide substitutions can create false signatures of selection when omitted from analysis \citep{venkat2018_mnm,dunn2019_mnm,lucaci2023_mhs}. Therefore, the simulator should include a tunable multinucleotide mechanism, for example by allowing instantaneous doublet or triplet changes with probability controlled by $\eta_g$.

Additional forms of model misspecification should also be simulated, including branch-specific GC shifts, heterogeneous synonymous substitution rates, branch-specific codon-bias changes, heterotachy, local compositional heterogeneity, and annotation-like artifacts such as frame disruptions, terminal sequence truncation, or erroneous short insertions.

The final class is especially important because BUSTED-E showed that residual local alignment or annotation errors can dominate apparent evidence for positive selection \citep{selberg2025_bustede}. Hard-negative families with no adaptive events but strong artifact pressure should therefore be included deliberately.

\subsection{Truth labels and metadata}

For each family, the simulator writes a structured truth object with family labels (presence or absence of adaptive events), branch labels (branches containing one or more adaptive events), latent-homology labels (truly adaptive branch--homology pairs), artifact labels (deliberately injected artifact classes), nuisance labels (recombination, indel burden, synonymous-rate variation, multinucleotide burden, GC shifts, and related covariates together with latent and observed saturation summaries), and optional identifiability labels such as minimal divergence around adaptive sites, post-indel observability, or homology fragmentation.

The truth object is the reference against which all supervised training and benchmarking are defined.

\section{Alignment ensemble generation}

\subsection{Rationale}

A single simulated family should produce multiple competing alignments. Let $\mathcal{A} = \{a_1,\dots,a_K\}$ denote the set of aligners. In the production-fast implementation I recommend
\[
\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{fast}} = \{\texttt{identity}, \texttt{BABAPPAlign}, \texttt{MAFFT}, \texttt{MUSCLE}\},
\]
with version numbers recorded explicitly at runtime and with method-level quarantine before tensorization. MAFFT and MUSCLE provide fast, independent production aligner channels, while \texttt{BABAPPAlign} supplies a distinct protein-model-guided codon-alignment channel \citep{katoh2013_mafft,edgar2004_muscle}. PRANK, T-Coffee, PAGAN, and related phylogeny-aware or consistency-based workflows remain valuable diagnostic or held-out-aligner comparisons because they differ in gap treatment, phylogeny awareness, and alignment heuristics \citep{notredame2000_tcoffee,loytynoja2012_pagan,loytynoja2014_prank}. If additional aligners are added later, the framework should support held-out-aligner evaluation so that generalization to unseen alignment algorithms can be measured.

In the current executable release, external aligners are optional rather than mandatory dependencies. The command \texttt{check-aligners} detects internal \texttt{identity}, internal \texttt{codon\_dropout}, MAFFT, PRANK, \texttt{BABAPPAlign}, MUSCLE, and T-Coffee backends when available. The command \texttt{align-external} can run a mixed internal/external ensemble, validate codon-frame compatibility, record failures, and quarantine methods whose family-level failure or site-map error rates exceed policy thresholds. \texttt{codon\_dropout} remains useful as an unmappable noise-control channel, but it is quarantined from mapped oracle-label training because taxon-specific codon gaps can create consensus site-map conflicts. The default mapped external-aligner validation plan now uses \texttt{identity}, MAFFT, \texttt{BABAPPAlign}, and MUSCLE with automatic quarantine. PRANK remains diagnostic-only because it was slow and produced real high-tier frame errors during external validation, while T-Coffee remains optional diagnostic-only because of expected runtime cost.

\subsection{Protein-guided and codon-aware workflows}

For coding sequences, each aligner should be run in codon-aware fashion whenever possible. When an aligner is protein-based, the recommended workflow is translation of CDS to protein, protein alignment, back-translation to codon alignment, and codon-phase validation with stop-codon artifact removal.
For aligners capable of direct codon alignment, the direct codon mode should also be tested.

Each family should therefore produce multiple alignment channels, including direct codon alignment, protein-guided codon alignment, an optional trimmed version, and an optional guide-tree-perturbed version.
This induces a richer alignment ensemble than merely changing the aligner binary.

\subsection{Cross-aligner homology projection during training}

Because simulated data provide latent homology identifiers, each alignment column can be projected back to the latent homology map. Let $c$ index a column produced by aligner $a$. Then
\[
M_{g,a}(c) \subseteq \mathcal{H}_g
\]
denotes the set of latent homology identifiers represented in that column. During training, columns that predominantly map to the same latent homology identifier can be tied by a consistency loss. This is the key device that allows a model to learn from conflicting alignments without pretending those alignments are identical.

For empirical data, latent homology identifiers are unknown. In deployment mode, site-level outputs are therefore reported conservatively: aligner-specific site scores are retained, and a cross-aligner consensus site score is reported only when correspondence is sufficiently stable. Branch-level and gene-level predictions remain the primary inferential outputs.

The implemented site-map layer operationalizes this requirement for simulation-supervised training. For every aligned codon column, \texttt{build-site-map} walks each taxon sequence against the original simulated FASTA and records the mapping
\[
\texttt{aligned\_codon\_site\_index}
\leftrightarrow
\texttt{true\_simulated\_site\_index}.
\]
Each mapping row records whether the correspondence is unique, all-gap, conflicting, or frame-invalid, together with a mapping-confidence score. Site-label extraction can then operate in mapped mode: the supervised target $y_{\mathrm{site}}$ is attached to the aligned site only when the aligned column maps back to a trusted original simulated site. This prevents label corruption when real aligners insert gaps or shift codon columns.

\section{Tensorization and input representation}

\subsection{Per-aligner tensor representation}

For family $g$, aligner $a$, and alignment length $L_{g,a}$ over $n_g$ taxa, construct a tensor
\[
X_{g,a} \in \mathbb{R}^{n_g \times L_{g,a} \times C},
\]
where channels $C$ may include codon token embeddings, amino-acid token embeddings, gap indicators, codon degeneracy classes, per-column entropy and gap fraction, taxon-specific missing-data masks, optional alignment-confidence scores, guide-tree distance features, windowed saturation-proxy features (for example codon-position-specific entropy, transition/transversion flattening, or corrected-versus-uncorrected distance residuals), and branch-incidence masks for queried branches.

In addition to per-column channels, each family should provide global or segment-level covariates that summarize information loss beyond any single column. These may include latent saturation summaries during training, observed saturation proxies available at deployment time, pairwise divergence quantiles, codon-position-specific distance summaries, and window-level estimates of synonymous exhaustion. Such covariates can be concatenated to branch-query embeddings or supplied to late pooling layers so that the network can learn when apparently strong local evidence should be discounted because it arises in a globally saturated background.

The implemented tensor substrate is intentionally smaller than this final representation. Version \ProjectVersion{} writes one tensor shard per family and alignment method with shape taxa $\times$ codons $\times$ channels, where the current channels are codon ID and gap indicator. Separate JSON metadata and label files preserve family ID, method, gene-level label, selected-site summaries, foreground taxon where applicable, saturation tier, and mapped-site information when an external site map is supplied. The site-dataset layer then extracts one row per labeled codon site, including safe numeric site features, split/method/tier metadata, and the supervised target \texttt{y\_site}. Truth-derived fields such as \texttt{selected\_sites}, \texttt{n\_selected\_sites}, and \texttt{y\_site} are explicitly excluded from predictive features by leakage audits and feature-selection rules.

The model should also receive a tree representation. A practical choice is a branch-incidence matrix or graph object:
\[
G_g = (V_g,E_g,\Phi_g),
\]
with node or edge attributes such as branch length, depth from root, descendant taxon count, and optional local-tree flags in recombinant segments.

\subsection{Branch-conditioned prediction}

Since the inferential target is branch-site selection, the network must be conditioned on a queried branch. For each branch $b$, define a branch embedding vector
\[
\mathbf{q}_{g,b} = f_{\mathrm{branch}}(T_g,b),
\]
where $f_{\mathrm{branch}}$ may encode branch length, topological depth, subtree size, sister-branch context, and whether the branch belongs to a recombinant segment.

The network then predicts:
\[
p^{(\mathrm{site})}_{g,a,b,c}, \quad
p^{(\mathrm{branch})}_{g,b}, \quad
p^{(\mathrm{gene})}_{g}, \quad
p^{(\mathrm{artifact})}_{g,a,b,c}, \quad
\hat{s}^{(\mathrm{risk})}_{g,b}, \quad
p^{(\mathrm{ood})}_{g}.
\]

\section{Neural architecture}

\subsection{Design goals}

The architecture should satisfy five requirements: it must capture local column-wise and codon-wise patterns relevant to branch-site selection, incorporate explicit phylogenetic branch context, separate alignment-invariant signal from aligner-specific artifact, aggregate evidence across multiple alignments of the same family, and recognize saturation-driven information loss with support for abstention when branch-site localization becomes unreliable.

\subsection{Proposed architecture}

I recommend a four-module design.

\subsubsection*{Module I: per-aligner sequence encoder}

Each alignment $X_{g,a}$ is passed through a per-aligner encoder $E_\theta$ shared across aligners:
\[
H_{g,a} = E_\theta(X_{g,a}) \in \mathbb{R}^{L_{g,a}\times d}.
\]
A practical implementation is a stack of 1D convolutional blocks followed by a lightweight Transformer or gated attention layers. Convolutions capture local codon and gap motifs; attention captures longer-range dependencies.

\subsubsection*{Module II: branch query conditioning}

The branch embedding $\mathbf{q}_{g,b}$ is used in cross-attention against column embeddings:
\[
U_{g,a,b} = \mathrm{CrossAttn}\!\left(\mathbf{q}_{g,b}, H_{g,a}\right),
\]
producing branch-conditioned column features. This makes the same alignment interpretable differently when querying different branches.

\subsubsection*{Module III: invariant versus artifact decomposition}

To encourage the model to separate true biological signal from aligner-specific bias, each branch-conditioned embedding is decomposed into two latent streams:
\[
U_{g,a,b} \rightarrow \left(S_{g,a,b}, R_{g,a,b}\right),
\]
where $S$ is intended to capture shared evolutionary signal and $R$ captures aligner-specific residual structure. A gradient-reversal aligner-adversary is attached to $S$ so that $S$ becomes less predictive of aligner identity, while an artifact head is attached to $R$ to explicitly model misalignment-like behavior. A parallel saturation-risk head receives pooled summaries from both streams together with global saturation covariates, because extreme saturation can destroy information even when aligner agreement is superficially high. This is not simply a regularization trick; it operationalizes the central scientific claim that robust branch-site signal should survive across multiple aligners more readily than artifacts do, but also that even consensus-like patterns should be down-weighted when the family resides in a saturation regime known to erase historical information.

\subsubsection*{Module IV: cross-aligner consensus aggregator}

For a queried branch, each aligner yields a set of site logits and reliability scores. These are combined by a learned reliability-weighted pooling step:
\[
\tilde{z}_{g,b,h} = \sum_{a=1}^{K} w_{g,a,b,h} z_{g,a,b,h},
\qquad
w_{g,a,b,h} = \frac{\exp(r_{g,a,b,h})}{\sum_{a'} \exp(r_{g,a',b,h})}.
\]
Here $h$ indexes latent homology identifiers during training; during deployment, $h$ is replaced by conservative cross-aligner consensus sites when available.

Branch-level and gene-level probabilities are then produced by pooling over sites and branches:
\[
p^{(\mathrm{branch})}_{g,b} = 1 - \prod_{h}(1-\sigma(\tilde{z}_{g,b,h})),
\qquad
p^{(\mathrm{gene})}_{g} = 1 - \prod_{b}(1-p^{(\mathrm{branch})}_{g,b}),
\]
or by learned pooling layers if empirically superior.

The current neural implementation records an important design correction. Early direct gene-level models, including a contrastive neural-v2 classifier and later saturation-aware and ranking-aware variants, were useful diagnostic tools but were not stable on four-tier saturation benchmarks. Their probabilities frequently compressed near 0.5 and repeated-seed test AUROC remained close to random. Label-signal auditing then showed that the learnable signal was sparse and site-localized. The implemented production research layer therefore pivots to \texttt{SiteMLPClassifier}, a small optional-PyTorch multilayer perceptron trained on leakage-audited site features. Gene-level support is no longer learned directly from a whole alignment tensor; it is derived by aggregating site-level probabilities, with maximum site probability serving as the default gene score. This is not yet the final branch-conditioned architecture, but it is the first implemented layer whose statistical target matches the biological sparsity of branch-site selection.

\subsection{Interpretability outputs}

The model should write interpretable auxiliary outputs for every prediction, including branch-specific saliency maps, per-aligner site scores, cross-aligner consensus site scores, artifact probability tracks, saturation diagnostic tracks with family-level saturation risk, uncertainty or out-of-distribution scores, and branch-level evidence decomposition by aligner.

These outputs are necessary if the method is to be used seriously by molecular evolution researchers rather than merely as a black-box classifier.

\section{Training objective and calibration}

\subsection{Multi-task loss}

The full loss function is
\[
\mathcal{L} =
\lambda_1 \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{site}} +
\lambda_2 \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{branch}} +
\lambda_3 \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{gene}} +
\lambda_4 \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{artifact}} +
\lambda_5 \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{consistency}} +
\lambda_6 \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{saturation}} +
\lambda_7 \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{calibration}} +
\lambda_8 \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{adv}}.
\]

The terms are defined as follows.

\textbf{Site loss.} Binary cross-entropy or focal loss on true branch--homology labels.

\textbf{Branch loss.} Binary cross-entropy on whether each branch contains at least one adaptive site.

\textbf{Gene loss.} Binary cross-entropy on whether a family contains any adaptive event.

\textbf{Artifact loss.} Binary cross-entropy for injected artifact classes and hard negatives.

\textbf{Consistency loss.} For columns across aligners that map to the same latent homology identifier, encourage similar site logits:
\[
\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{consistency}}
=
\sum_{(a,c),(a',c') \mapsto h}
\left\lVert
z_{g,a,b,c} - z_{g,a',b,c'}
\right\rVert_1 .
\]

\textbf{Saturation loss.} Regression or ordinal-classification loss for latent saturation burden and observed saturation tier. A practical implementation is to predict both a continuous latent saturation summary and a coarse class label (low/moderate/high/extreme), using mean-squared error plus cross-entropy. This auxiliary loss encourages the shared representation to encode information-destruction regimes rather than confusing them with positive selection.

\textbf{Calibration loss.} Brier score or temperature-scaled negative log-likelihood computed on a held-out calibration set.

\textbf{Adversarial loss.} A gradient-reversal aligner-classification loss to reduce aligner-specific information in the invariant latent stream.

\subsection{Training data strata}

The simulator should generate at least six training strata: clean null (no adaptive events with mild nuisance processes), clean positive (adaptive events without severe nuisance pressure), hard null (no adaptive events but strong confounders designed to fool classical methods), noisy positive (adaptive events mixed with indels, recombination, saturation, SRV, and MNMs), a saturation ladder spanning low/moderate/high/extreme tiers for matched null and positive families, and ambiguous or out-of-distribution regimes that are deliberately more extreme than the core training distribution.

This stratification is essential. If the model sees only clean positives and clean negatives, it cannot learn the artifact boundary that matters most.

\subsection{Data splitting}

Train, validation, calibration, and test splits must be family-disjoint. No family seed, local tree, or latent truth object may appear in more than one split. I recommend:
\[
80\% \text{ train},\quad 10\% \text{ validation},\quad 5\% \text{ calibration},\quad 5\% \text{ test},
\]
with optional dedicated stress-test sets.

This splitting policy is implemented in \texttt{babappa index-dataset} and in the multi-source merger \texttt{babappa merge-datasets}. The default implementation assigns all methods from the same family to the same split, preventing leakage between alignment scaffolds of the same simulated history. Merged multi-saturation datasets use source-qualified family identifiers so that identically named families from different saturation-tier panels do not collide.

At least three special hold-outs should be created: a held-out aligner split in which one aligner or workflow is excluded from training, a held-out nuisance regime covering extreme SRV/recombination/indel burden or unseen saturation conditions, and a held-out tree regime with different taxon counts or tree shapes.

\subsection{Probability calibration and decision thresholds}

Raw neural probabilities are rarely perfectly calibrated. Therefore \ProjectName{} should use a dedicated calibration split to fit temperature scaling or isotonic regression for branch- and gene-level outputs. Site-level outputs may also be calibrated, but because site predictions are more variable, the default reporting should prioritize branch- and gene-level calibrated probabilities.

Decision thresholds should be derived empirically on the calibration split with explicit false-discovery control against null and hard-negative families. A practical deployment policy is to report a continuous calibrated probability, a conservative binary call only above a threshold chosen to control empirical false discovery on hard negatives, and an abstain flag when either $p^{(\mathrm{ood})}$ exceeds a prespecified limit or saturation-risk signals indicate that information loss has crossed a preregistered boundary for reliable branch-site localization.

The current implementation supports calibration and operating-point selection at both site and aggregated gene levels. Gene-level threshold profiling remains available through \texttt{threshold-policy}, while \texttt{calibrate-site-neural}, \texttt{site-threshold-policy}, and \texttt{aggregation-threshold-policy} implement the corresponding site-level and site-to-gene decision layers. Temperature scaling and a lightweight quantile calibration option are implemented for site neural predictions. In the 10,000-family site-level benchmark, quantile calibration substantially improved calibration error relative to temperature scaling, although strict site-level FDR remained difficult because individual selected sites are rare. This motivates reporting both continuous site probabilities and aggregation-level discovery profiles rather than treating a single site-level FDR threshold as the only scientifically relevant operating point.

\section{Benchmarking and evaluation}

\subsection{Baselines}

\ProjectName{} should be benchmarked against both classical and uncertainty-aware baselines, including the CODEML branch-site test \citep{zhang2005,yang2007,alvarez2023_pamlguide}, MEME and BUSTED \citep{murrell2012_meme,murrell2015_busted}, aBSREL \citep{smith2015_absrel}, BUSTED-E \citep{selberg2025_bustede}, and BAli-Phy-style alignment-integrated analyses on subsets where computation is feasible \citep{redelings2014_erasing,redelings2021_baliphy3}.

Recombination-aware preprocessing using GARD may also be included where appropriate \citep{pond2006_gard,pond2006_automated}. Tree inference for alignment-based baselines may be performed with IQ-TREE 2 \citep{minh2020_iqtree2}. HyPhy 2.5 provides the baseline implementations for several episodic-selection tests \citep{pond2020_hyphy}.

\subsection{Primary metrics}

Evaluation must be reported at gene, branch, and site levels. Gene-level reporting should include AUROC, AUPRC, sensitivity, specificity, precision, Matthews correlation coefficient, Brier score or calibration error, and empirical false-discovery behavior on null and hard-negative sets. Branch-level reporting should include AUROC and AUPRC over branches, per-family branch ranking accuracy, top-$k$ hit rates for true-event branches, and branch-probability calibration. Site-level reporting should include AUROC and AUPRC on simulated truth, localization overlap for selected sites, and consensus-restricted cross-aligner site accuracy.

\subsection{Robustness metrics}

Because robustness to alignment uncertainty is a central claim, additional metrics are needed.

\textbf{Cross-aligner dispersion.} For family $g$ and branch $b$, define
\[
\Delta_{g,b} = \mathrm{Var}\!\left(p^{(\mathrm{branch},a)}_{g,b}: a=1,\dots,K\right).
\]
Lower dispersion at fixed accuracy indicates greater stability.

\textbf{Artifact discrimination.} Measure the ability to separate true positives from hard negatives containing severe misalignment-like patterns but no adaptive events.

\textbf{Held-out-aligner generalization.} Evaluate performance when one aligner or one workflow is absent from training.

\textbf{Saturation-specific evaluation.} Because saturation is a primary confounder, every main metric should be stratified by saturation tier. At minimum, report AUROC, AUPRC, empirical false discovery, recall, and abstention rate separately for low, moderate, high, and extreme saturation. In addition, quantify whether the saturation-risk head is monotone with latent multiple-hit burden and with observed proxies such as Iss-like statistics or entropy saturation scores. A scientifically desirable pattern is not constant sensitivity at all costs, but controlled degradation with increasing abstention in the most saturated regimes.

This evaluation layer is now executable at gene and site levels. The command \texttt{stratified-eval} computes gene-level metrics by split, saturation tier, method, and their combinations. The site-specific command \texttt{site-stratified-eval} applies the same principle to site predictions, and \texttt{aggregate-sites} evaluates whether site evidence recovers gene-level simulator labels. Version \ProjectVersion{} also includes repeated-seed site-neural stability benchmarking, site baseline versus neural comparison, aggregation null/decoy controls, leakage audits, and large-run planning. The completed 10,000-family four-tier benchmark shows high site-level AUROC in every saturation tier, with graceful degradation from low to extreme saturation rather than the probability-collapse behavior observed in direct gene-level models.

\textbf{Stress-test curves.} Plot performance as functions of recombination rate, indel burden, saturation, SRV intensity, and MNM burden.

\subsection{Statistical reporting}

All benchmarking on test sets should report confidence intervals from repeated simulation batches or bootstrap resampling. Multiple-hypothesis situations, such as evaluating many families or many branches in empirical applications, should use false discovery rate control \citep{benjamini1995}. The manuscript-ready reporting layer should record both raw and adjusted $P$-value analogs where external likelihood baselines are used.

