\section{Auxiliary ancestral reconstruction and protein-energy interpretation layer}

\subsection{Role within the overall framework}

The auxiliary layer is deliberately downstream of the primary \ProjectName{} classifier. Its purpose is to provide mechanistic interpretation for a restricted subset of families, branches, and sites that satisfy conservative branch-level criteria in the main framework. In the default workflow, a family is eligible only when calibrated family-level adaptive probability exceeds a prespecified threshold, at least one branch-level probability exceeds a stricter threshold, artifact probability remains below a conservative ceiling, no abstention or out-of-distribution flag is raised, and phylogenetic information is sufficient for ASR (for example minimum taxon count and branch support).

This design ensures that the auxiliary module does not dilute the central statistical target of the main paper. Instead, it asks a different question: \emph{given a branch-site candidate that is already robust to alignment uncertainty under the main \ProjectName{} framework, what structural or energetic interpretation is consistent with the reconstructed historical sequence changes?}

\subsection{Input objects and uncertainty propagation}

For each eligible family $g$, the auxiliary module consumes the alignment ensemble $\{X^{(a)}_g\}_{a=1}^{K}$, the chosen tree or tree ensemble, candidate branches from the primary model, site-level saliency or posterior-like importance scores from the primary model, and optional structural templates or structure-prediction metadata.

Because alignment uncertainty affects ASR as well as selection inference, the module must not reconstruct a single ancestor from a single alignment and treat it as ground truth. Instead, ancestral states should be represented as an ensemble:
\[
\mathcal{R}_{g,n} = \left\{R^{(a,m)}_{g,n}: a=1,\dots,K;\ m=1,\dots,M_a\right\},
\]
where $n$ indexes an internal node, $a$ indexes alignment workflow, and $m$ indexes posterior samples, bootstrap replicates, or method-specific alternative reconstructions. Alignment-integrated ASR has been shown to improve accuracy relative to single-alignment practice, and recent work suggests that richer generative models can improve ancestor recovery under some conditions \citep{aadland2020_asr,deleonardis2025_ardca}. At the same time, ASR appears to be more robust than often assumed in many realistic settings when phylogenetic signal is adequate \citep{muniztrejo2025_robustasr}. The auxiliary design therefore treats uncertainty propagation as a requirement, not an optional refinement.

\subsection{Recommended ASR implementation modes}

The software should support at least three ASR modes.

\textbf{Mode A: per-aligner classical ASR.} Each alignment is analyzed independently using a classical maximum-likelihood or Bayesian ASR engine. Nodewise posterior probabilities are exported for all residues of interest.

\textbf{Mode B: alignment-integrated ASR.} Reconstructions are combined across alignments with weighting by alignment confidence, posterior probability, or equal weights. The alignment-integrated strategy is strongly motivated by prior work showing improved ancestral accuracy and better downstream structural and functional inference \citep{aadland2020_asr}.

\textbf{Mode C: generative or deep ASR.} A modern sequence model with explicit generative capacity is used as an alternative or complement to classical ASR, particularly when epistasis or family-specific constraints are expected to matter \citep{deleonardis2025_ardca}. This mode should remain optional and clearly versioned because such methods are still comparatively new.

For each internal node $n$ and site $j$, the output should be a residue distribution rather than only a single MAP call:
\[
P(A_{g,n,j}=r \mid \text{data}) \quad \text{for} \quad r \in \mathcal{R},
\]
together with an entropy-like uncertainty summary.

\subsection{Branchwise substitution-set construction}

The main \ProjectName{} model nominates branch-level candidates and may optionally prioritize a subset of high-importance sites. For a focal branch $b=(u\rightarrow v)$, the auxiliary layer constructs a branchwise substitution set
\[
\mathcal{S}_{g,b} = \left\{(j, r_u, r_v, w_{j})\right\},
\]
where $j$ is the site or homologous region identifier, $r_u$ and $r_v$ are ancestor and descendant residues sampled or summarized from the reconstruction ensemble, and $w_j$ is a weight derived from primary-model site support, ASR confidence, or both. In practice, the set may be restricted to sites with high primary-model attribution, low posterior ambiguity under ASR, and placement within domains or structural regions of interest.

This substitution set defines the mechanistic object that will be evaluated downstream. Importantly, it is not interpreted as proof of adaptation. It is interpreted as the collection of historical amino-acid changes most plausibly associated with a robust branch-site event.

\subsection{Protein structure and energy proxies}

The auxiliary module should support a pluggable structural-analysis backend. Because the exact engine may evolve during project execution, this manuscript leaves the implementation-specific program name as a placeholder, but requires for each reconstructed sequence or sampled ancestor a predicted three-dimensional structure or structure ensemble, residue-level confidence metrics, a global stability-like score, residuewise or regionwise energetic frustration or strain proxies, interface or binding-surface scores where applicable, and optional conformational or flexibility proxies.

For each focal branch, the module should compare ancestor and descendant ensembles and estimate a distribution of energetic changes:
\[
\Delta E_{g,b}^{(s)} = E\!\left(\text{descendant}^{(s)}\right) - E\!\left(\text{ancestor}^{(s)}\right),
\]
or analogous quantities for any chosen score $E$. The goal is not to recover an absolute physical free energy with experimental precision, but to obtain a coherent comparative proxy for whether the nominated substitutions are associated with stability-like, interface-like, or conformationally informative shifts. Energy-landscape thinking is increasingly recognized as a useful way to interpret ASR-based evolutionary studies, particularly when substitutions alter conformational access or functional constraints rather than simply increasing or decreasing stability \citep{chisholm2024_energy}.

\subsection{Outputs of the auxiliary layer}

For each eligible family and branch, the auxiliary module should produce an ASR uncertainty report for focal nodes, a weighted list of candidate historical substitutions, branchwise distributions of stability-like or energy-like changes, sitewise structural annotations mapping candidate substitutions onto domains/interfaces/cores/surfaces or predicted flexible regions, and an interpretation summary (for example predominantly stability-like, predominantly interface-like, conformationally suggestive, or mechanistically unresolved).

The final report should clearly label all such summaries as \emph{auxiliary mechanistic interpretation}. They are not to be combined with the main adaptive probability into one opaque composite score unless a later study explicitly validates such integration.

\subsection{Evaluation of the auxiliary layer}

The auxiliary module should be evaluated separately from the primary selection detector, with targets including ASR accuracy on simulated ancestors, robustness of ASR summaries across alignment workflows, rank correlation between true simulated energetic perturbations and predicted branchwise energy proxies when latent quantities are available, localization accuracy for structurally causal substitutions in simulation studies with explicit energy- or structure-linked fitness effects, and empirical case studies anchored in literature-supported mechanistic hypotheses.

In other words, the auxiliary layer is validated as a \emph{mechanistic interpretation module}, not as the principal branch-site selection statistic.


\section{Planned empirical workflow}

Although the present article does not yet include executed empirical results, the intended empirical workflow is straightforward. It begins with curated orthogroups that have verified coding sequences, followed by alignment-ensemble generation for each orthogroup. Next, an ML tree is inferred for each workflow or a trusted species-tree mapping protocol is applied. Before inference, empirical saturation diagnostics are computed using \texttt{babappa\_saturation.py} or the integrated saturation module.

The intended mature \ProjectName{} run will produce calibrated branch- and site-level probabilities, artifact probabilities, saturation-risk scores, and conservative gene-level support derived from site evidence. In the current executable implementation, the validated route is site-level scoring followed by site-to-gene aggregation; direct gene-level classifiers are retained mainly as diagnostic baselines. Classical and uncertainty-aware baseline methods should be run on the same orthogroups for direct comparison. Cross-method concordance should be evaluated, with special attention to cases that appear selection-like under classical methods but are classified as artifact-like or low-identifiability by \ProjectName{}.

The auxiliary ASR/energy layer is triggered only for robust, cross-aligner-stable, low-artifact candidates that pass branch-level confidence criteria. Focal ancestors are reconstructed under alignment-aware ensembles with propagated uncertainty, and branchwise structural or energy-like shifts are summarized. Final reports explicitly separate primary selection inference from auxiliary mechanistic interpretation.

