\subsection*{Current engineering benchmark status}

The current code base has passed end-to-end validation through simulation, saturation-panel construction, alignment scaffolding, external-alignment method policy, tensorization, dataset indexing, oracle site-label extraction, site-dataset construction, leakage auditing, site baseline training, site neural training, calibration, threshold-policy profiling, stratified evaluation, site-to-gene aggregation, aggregation null controls, repeated-seed stability benchmarking, report generation, cross-tier summarization, and run-summary validation. The most important scientific correction made during development was the shift away from direct whole-gene binary classification.

The direct gene-level phase was informative but insufficient. A first 1000-family moderate-saturation neural run collapsed to essentially all-negative predictions at threshold 0.5 and all-split AUROC approximately 0.5165. A later gene-level neural-v2 model improved the moderate-saturation 1000-family setting, reaching all-split AUROC approximately 0.7886 and test AUROC approximately 0.7390, but four-tier saturation tests showed unstable ranking and probability compression. Saturation-aware embeddings, group-weighted losses, balanced samplers, ranking losses, and site-attention variants did not make direct gene-level classification robust. Label-signal auditing then showed that the biologically meaningful signal was sparse and site-localized.

The implemented site-level formulation resolves this dilution problem in the current simulation setting. In the completed 10,000-family four-tier benchmark, oracle selected-site labels were extracted from simulator truth, leakage-prone truth metadata were excluded from input features, and a downsampled site dataset with 1,645,380 rows was used for site-level learning. The site neural model achieved all-split AUROC 0.9817 and test AUROC 0.9831. Tier-specific AUROC values remained high but declined gracefully with saturation pressure: low 0.9934, moderate 0.9878, high 0.9749, and extreme 0.9515. Repeated-seed stability was strong, with mean test AUROC approximately 0.9789 and standard deviation approximately 0.0019. Aggregating site probabilities by maximum site probability recovered gene-level simulator labels with AUROC 1.0 and F1 1.0 across split, method, and saturation tiers. Null and decoy aggregation controls remained near random, with control AUROC means approximately 0.50--0.54 and empirical $p=0.0196$.

The fast external-aligner 10,000-family validation is also complete across low, moderate, high, and extreme saturation tiers. Each tier contains 2,500 simulated families and uses the production-fast ensemble \texttt{identity}, MAFFT, \texttt{BABAPPAlign}, and MUSCLE with automatic method quarantine. Low-tier \texttt{BABAPPAlign} was quarantined because 38 of 2,500 family-method attempts failed, giving a failure fraction of 0.0152 above the 0.01 policy threshold; \texttt{identity}, MAFFT, and MUSCLE remained usable. In moderate, high, and extreme tiers, all four production-fast methods passed method policy. Site-neural test AUROC values were 0.9945, 0.9835, 0.9726, and 0.9417 for low, moderate, high, and extreme tiers, respectively. Site-to-gene test AUROC values were 1.0, 0.9999, 1.0, and 1.0. Aggregation controls remained near-random, with shuffled-label control means approximately 0.50 and random or permutation controls approximately 0.53, while observed aggregation AUROC remained approximately 0.997--1.0.

The conservative explicit branch-truth 100,000-family Apple Silicon/MPS validation is now also complete across low, moderate, high, and extreme saturation tiers. Each tier contains 25,000 simulated families with 8 taxa and 300 codons, uses the production-fast ensemble \texttt{identity}, MAFFT, \texttt{BABAPPAlign}, and MUSCLE, and applies the \texttt{conservative\_branch\_site} feature policy. The plan completed 104 tier-stage markers with no partial markers. Truth-status audit reported \texttt{explicit\_truth\_ok}, \texttt{explicit\_simulator\_branch\_truth}, and \texttt{proxy\_from\_foreground\_taxon=False} for every tier. Branch-site neural test AUROC values were 0.9986, 0.9970, 0.9945, and 0.9900 for low, moderate, high, and extreme tiers, respectively. Branch-level aggregation all-split AUROC values were 0.9998, 0.9994, 0.9988, and 0.9979. Branch-to-gene aggregation all-split AUROC values were 0.9997, 0.9996, 0.9995, and 0.9994. Global shuffled-branch-label controls remained near random at approximately 0.499 AUROC, whereas some prevalence-preserving or family-preserving controls remained high and should be interpreted as partial rather than fully destructive controls.

These results should be interpreted as simulation-oracle branch-site validation, not as final empirical branch-site \ProjectName{} performance. The target $y_{\mathrm{branch,site}}$ is allowed as a supervised label in simulation, but it is never allowed as an input feature. The branch and branch-to-gene aggregation results show that sparse branch-site evidence can recover higher-level simulator support under the current simulation design. The completed external-aligner validations support the feasibility of the production-fast method set \texttt{identity}, MAFFT, \texttt{BABAPPAlign}, and MUSCLE with quarantine. PRANK should remain diagnostic-only, and T-Coffee should remain optional diagnostic-only. The warning \texttt{branch\_context\_ablation:context\_only\_shortcut\_high} remains important and motivates conservative feature policy, ablation reporting, and cautious empirical deployment.

