\subsection*{Empirical claim boundary}

The empirical bridge deliberately separates software success from biological discovery. No simulator truth is available or used during empirical inference. \texttt{WRKY\_candidate\_01} demonstrated that BABAPPA can abstain from a saturated, divergent plant WRKY panel: the family had mean p-distance 0.725799 and was treated as an out-of-domain failure-mode probe. \texttt{WRKY\_candidate\_02\_close} demonstrated that the same pipeline can process a closer Brassicaceae-like WRKY panel end to end: 7 taxa, approximately 490 codons, aligned mean p-distance near 0.101293, accepted OOD prefilter, in-domain applicability, moderate-tier model selection, MPS scoring, diagnostic-positive class, maximum gene support 0.177189, and 6,954 called branch-site rows. These outputs are guarded simulation-trained diagnostic evidence. They are not manuscript-ready empirical positive-selection claims until codeml/HyPhy-style reference comparison, matched-null calibration, and close-taxa negative controls have been completed and interpreted.

\section{Discussion}

The central claim of \ProjectName{} is not that deep learning replaces codon models by fiat, nor that simulator-trained classifiers can recover all historical adaptive events. Rather, the claim is that robust branch-site inference requires explicit exposure to the same nuisance processes that undermine conventional analyses, together with a training objective that rewards alignment-invariant signal and penalizes aligner-specific artifact. This is a narrower claim than ``MSA-free selection detection,'' but it is a more defensible and immediately actionable one.

Several aspects of the framework deserve emphasis. Using multiple alignments for the same simulated family is a methodological necessity, not a cosmetic ensemble trick, because it forces direct confrontation with homology uncertainty. Explicit latent-homology tracking also gives the supervised objective a rigorous definition that empirical data cannot provide directly. Hard-negative artifact families are as important as positive families, because they teach the model when selection-like patterns should be rejected.

Calibration and abstention are equally central. Researchers need to know not only when the model predicts selection, but also when the data are too confounded or too far outside the training domain for a reliable claim. This point is especially important for saturation. A trustworthy method should explicitly indicate when deep multiple-hit accumulation has destroyed branch-site identifiability, rather than returning an overconfident score.

I also argue that incorporating the second direction as an auxiliary layer is methodologically superior to presenting it as an independent primary selection framework. The recent ASR and protein-energy literature makes that distinction important. Alignment-aware ASR can improve downstream inference, energy-landscape analysis can illuminate how evolutionary changes reshape stability or conformational preferences, and newer generative ASR strategies may capture epistatic structure more effectively \citep{aadland2020_asr,chisholm2024_energy,deleonardis2025_ardca}. However, none of these developments implies that structural or energetic change alone is a statistically sufficient test of positive selection. By keeping ASR and energy-landscape analysis explicitly downstream of the primary branch-site detector, \ProjectName{} preserves a clean separation between \emph{evidence that a robust adaptive-like signal is present} and \emph{hypotheses about what that signal may have done mechanistically}. In my view, that separation makes the paper stronger and more reviewer-resistant.

The framework also has limitations. Any simulator, no matter how rich, remains a stylized approximation to reality. Overfitting to simulator peculiarities is therefore a major risk, and held-out nuisance regimes, held-out aligners, and empirical stress testing are essential. Site-level localization on empirical data remains intrinsically harder than gene-level or branch-level prediction because latent homology cannot be observed directly. Moreover, a model that is robust to alignment uncertainty is not automatically robust to errors in orthogroup construction, hidden paralogy, or annotation mistakes; these should be treated as additional future nuisance classes. The auxiliary module introduces its own limitations as well: ASR uncertainty, structure-prediction uncertainty, and imperfect correspondence between energetic proxies and organismal fitness all mean that mechanistic summaries must remain explicitly probabilistic and interpretive.

The current software status adds a practical limitation to this conceptual list. The implemented neural model is now site-level and branch-site-level rather than direct gene-level, and the completed controlled, fast external-aligner, and conservative explicit branch-truth benchmarks support the claim that the oracle-supervised formulation is learnable and stable under four-tier saturation pressure. However, this remains simulation-supervised method development. The target labels are derived from simulator truth, and the current branch-to-gene aggregation result is an upper-bound validation of the information pathway rather than an empirical proof of selection inference. The external-aligner result validates a feasible production-fast ensemble of \texttt{identity}, MAFFT, \texttt{BABAPPAlign}, and MUSCLE with automatic quarantine. PRANK and T-Coffee should remain diagnostic-only unless explicitly used for stress testing. The empirical bridge, pilot-panel tooling, OOD gate, and deployable model package are now implemented, but broad real-world performance, calibrated empirical p-like values, and concordance with codeml/HyPhy-style reference workflows remain unproven. The context-only shortcut warning observed in branch-context ablation should remain a visible caveat whenever branch-conditioned results are reported.

Despite these limits, \ProjectName{} provides a coherent path toward a serious new class of methods in molecular evolution. Instead of relying on one alignment and one codon model, it operationalizes a stricter principle: infer adaptive signal only after confronting alternative homology hypotheses and major confounders in a supervised, calibrated, and benchmarked framework. The auxiliary ASR/energy layer then extends the workflow from detection to interpretation without collapsing distinct inferential levels into a single score.

The present conclusion is therefore bounded. BABAPPA is now a validated research-alpha framework with explicit simulator branch-site truth validation at 100,000 families, a deployable simulation-trained conservative model package, and a guarded empirical diagnostic workflow. It has proven scalable explicit-truth simulation validation, conservative feature-policy robustness, feasible production alignment ensembles, model packaging, and empirical OOD abstention. It has not proven final empirical positive-selection inference, broad real-world performance, codeml/HyPhy concordance, or calibrated empirical p-values. Future manuscript claims should require simulation-matched calibration, close-taxa negative controls, and externally interpretable reference comparisons before any empirical discovery statement is made.
